refined_question:
Let Ω be a bounded spatial domain with periodic boundary conditions, and for a detection kernel g of perceptual radius R define f_{g,R}(x,t) = ∫_Ω f(y,t) g(x−y) dy. Consider the three consumer-resource formulations from §2.2.2:
(2.14) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇v_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v);
(2.18) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇q_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v),  q_t = bv − μq;
(2.19) u_t = D1Δu − γ∇·(u∇q_{g,R}) + cβuv/(α+v) − du,  v_t = D2Δv + rv(1−v/K) − βuv/(α+v),  q_t = buv − (μ+ξu)q,
where u is consumer density, v is resource density, q is the cognitive map, γ,r,K,β,α,d,c,b are positive, and μ,ξ are nonnegative. Assuming spatially non-constant steady-state solutions exist, how do their qualitative features differ among (2.14), (2.18), and (2.19)? In particular, how does replacing direct perception of current resource density by a dynamic cognitive map that grows either with v alone or with uv and decays at rate μ or μ+ξu alter the resulting spatially non-constant steady-state profiles?
